Integrating Jira with your planning poker site is a total game-changer. It eliminates the manual "copy-paste" drag of ticket titles and descriptions, keeping the momentum high.

To do this in C# / ASP.NET, you’ll use the Jira REST API v3. Since you are on a Razor stack, you can handle the API logic in your C# controller/service and pass the "Pointless" stories to your view.

1. The JQL (Jira Query Language)
The "magic" happens in the JQL query. To find stories in a specific sprint that have no points and aren't bugs or spikes, your query will look like this:

project = "YOUR_PROJ" AND sprint = {sprintId} AND issuetype = "Story" AND "Story Points" is EMPTY

Note: If you want all types except bugs/spikes, use:
sprint = {sprintId} AND issuetype NOT IN ("Bug", "Spike") AND "Story Points" is EMPTY

2. Setting Up Authentication
You shouldn't use your password. Instead:

Go to Atlassian Account Settings > Security > API Tokens.

Create a token and save it.

In C#, you will use Basic Auth, which is a Base64 encoded string of your-email@example.com:your-api-token.

3. The C# Implementation (HttpClient)
Here is a concise way to fetch these issues using System.Net.Http.

C#
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;

public async Task<List<JiraIssue>> GetUnpointedStories(string sprintId)
{
    var email = "your-email@example.com";
    var apiToken = "your-api-token";
    var domain = "your-domain.atlassian.net";
    
    // 1. Encode Credentials
    var authHeader = Convert.ToBase64String(Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes($"{email}:{apiToken}"));

    using var client = new HttpClient();
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri($"https://{domain}/");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Basic", authHeader);

    // 2. Build JQL
    var jql = $"sprint = {sprintId} AND issuetype = 'Story' AND 'Story Points' is EMPTY";
    var url = $"/rest/api/3/search?jql={Uri.EscapeDataString(jql)}&fields=summary,description,issuetype";

    // 3. Execute
    var response = await client.GetAsync(url);
    if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
    {
        var json = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
        // Parse the 'issues' array from the JSON response
        var result = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<JiraResponse>(json);
        return result.Issues;
    }
    
    return new List<JiraIssue>();
}
4. Handling "Story Points" Custom Fields
Jira is notorious for using Custom Field IDs for story points. While many Cloud instances support the literal string "Story Points", some require the ID (e.g., customfield_10016).

Tip: To find your specific ID, go to a ticket that has points in your browser and append .json to the URL. Search the page (Ctrl+F) for the number of points to find the field key.

5. Connecting to your Razor/JS Frontend
Once your C# code pulls the list, you can pass it to your Razor Page and use Javascript to "Push" them into your planning poker lobby:

Razor Controller: Calls GetUnpointedStories and returns a JsonResult.

JavaScript: Fetches that JSON when the "Import Sprint" button is clicked.

SignalR (Optional): Since you're building a live site, use SignalR to broadcast the new "Deck" of stories to all connected players simultaneously.

🚀 Pro Gamification Idea: "The Import Animation"
Since your site is all about fun, don't just "show" a list. When the user imports stories from Jira:

The "Suck-In": Show a Jira logo portal opening up.

The "Vacuum": Have the stories fly out of the portal and onto the poker table as physical-looking cards.

The "Dust-Off": Since these stories have "no points," they could appear dusty or greyed out until the first person votes.

