Markdown
# Feature Specification & Implementation Guide: Gamified Planning Poker
**Target Architecture:** C# ASP.NET Razor Pages, HTML5/CSS3, JavaScript (Frontend), SignalR (Real-time Sync)  
**Integrations:** Jira REST API v3  
**Purpose:** This document serves as a comprehensive system prompt and specification blueprint for a coding AI to design, structure, and generate code for an ultra-gamified Planning Poker web application.

---

## 1. System Overview & Tech Stack Context
When implementing these features, ensure adherence to this architectural blueprint:
* **Backend:** ASP.NET Core MVC / Razor Pages (C#), utilizing `HttpClient` for API integrations and dependency injection for service lifecycles.
* **Real-time Engine:** ASP.NET Core SignalR for bidirectional, low-latency state synchronization across the poker lobby (votes, timers, triggers, animations).
* **Frontend:** Vanilla JS or lightweight framework mapping to HTML5 Canvas / CSS3 animations (Lottie, native keyframes) for heavy performance-optimized animations.

---

## 2. Core Gamification & Fun Features

### 2.1 The "Lone Wolf" (Formerly Shame) & Counter-Spell
* **Mechanic:** Triggered when exactly one player holds out and breaks team consensus.
* **Visual FX:** A dramatic visual spotlight tracks down the holdout's avatar while a humorous visual cue (e.g., tumbling tumbleweeds or a cricket animation) triggers on screen.
* **The Counter-Spell Power-Up:** Once per session, the targeted "Lone Wolf" can activate a Counter-Spell, which instantly deflects the focus and turns all *other* players' avatars into chickens for 10 seconds.

### 2.2 The "Duel of Perspective" (Avatar Battles)
* **Trigger:** Triggered when the initial vote reveal shows wide disagreement (e.g., one user votes a `2` and another votes a `13`).
* **UI State:** The application transitions into a *Street Fighter*-style versus split-screen showing both avatars.
* **Interactive Elements:** While giving their structural arguments, both users can press rapid "Argument Hotkeys" (*"Legacy Code!"*, *"Pre-existing Template!"*, *"Unclear Requirements!"*) that cast visual attack or shield spells against the opposing avatar.

### 2.3 High-Value Tickets: "The Boss Fight"
* **Trigger:** When a ticket with a high estimate card (13, 21, or 100) is brought to the table.
* **Visual FX:** The backdrop transforms into a pixelated "Dungeon Boss Arena." The ticket transforms into a monster card with a health bar mapped to the level of disagreement.
* **Dynamic Resolution:** If the team breaks down the ticket into smaller sub-tasks, the monster visually splits into smaller, easily defeated minions. Reaching consensus executes a "Team Ultimate Move" where all avatars combine powers to blast the card into the "Done" pile.

### 2.4 Token Economy: "Scrum Gold" & The Prop Shop
* **Earning Loops:** * **Estimate Streaks:** Hitting consensus on the first reveal 3 times in a row triggers a "Godlike Consensus" animation and awards bonus Scrum Gold.
  * **The Oracle Prediction:** Before reveals, users can wager site currency on what the final consensus number will be. Accurate predictions reward "Oracle Points."
* **Utility:** Players spend Scrum Gold in a cosmetic "Prop Shop" to unlock avatar skins, custom hats, particle effects, or unique card backs.

### 2.5 Active Environment Elements
* **The Floor is Lava (Discussion Killer):** If a post-reveal debate surpasses 5 minutes, digital lava begins rising from the bottom of the UI. Avatars must automatically "jump" onto the closest UI buttons to escape. This acts as a high-visibility, lighthearted nudge to wrap up discussion and re-vote.
* **Anonymous Vibe Check:** Prior to starting the backlog, users click an emoji representing their sprint confidence (🚀, 😱, 😴). This displays an anonymous aggregate "Weather Report" to the facilitator.

### 2.6 End-of-Session Analytics & Awards
Generate an interactive post-game scoreboard summarizing player archetypes:
* **The Anchor:** Voted the exact final consensus value most frequently.
* **The Chaos Monkey:** Triggered the highest volume of Lone Wolf statuses or Avatar Battles.
* **The Speedster:** Lowest mean reaction time to lock in votes.
* **The Diplomat:** Highest frequency of pivoting their vote to achieve team alignment.

---

## 3. Jira Integration Module (C# Backend)

### 3.1 Objective
Connect to an active Atlassian Jira Cloud instance, pull the targeted Sprint's backlog, and filter for items that require estimation.

### 3.2 Target Criteria (JQL)
Extract stories matching:
1. Assigned to a specific Sprint ID.
2. Issue type is explicitly a "Story" (excluding Bugs, Spikes, Epics, or Tasks).
3. The Story Points field value is currently null/empty.

### 3.3 Core C# Service Implementation
Use the following structured class to handle the authenticated extraction via the Jira REST API v3:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class JiraIssueService
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;
    private readonly string _jiraDomain;
    private readonly string _authHeaderValue;

    public JiraIssueService(HttpClient httpClient, string email, string apiToken, string jiraDomain)
    {
        _httpClient = httpClient ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(httpClient));
        _jiraDomain = jiraDomain ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(jiraDomain));
        
        // Construct Basic Authentication string (Base64 encoded email:token)
        var credentials = $"{email}:{apiToken}";
        var bytes = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(credentials);
        _authHeaderValue = Convert.ToBase64String(bytes);
    }

    public async Task<List<JiraIssueDto>> GetUnpointedStoriesAsync(string sprintId)
    {
        _httpClient.BaseAddress = new Uri($"https://{_jiraDomain}/");
        _httpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Basic", _authHeaderValue);
        _httpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders.Accept.Add(new MediaTypeWithQualityHeaderValue("application/json"));

        // JQL Filter: Target specific sprint, ensure type is Story, verify Story Points are empty
        // NOTE: If your instance requires a custom field ID, substitute 'Story Points' with 'customfield_XXXXX'
        string jql = $"sprint = {sprintId} AND issuetype = 'Story' AND 'Story Points' is EMPTY";
        string fields = "summary,description,issuetype,status";
        string requestUrl = $"/rest/api/3/search?jql={Uri.EscapeDataString(jql)}&fields={Uri.EscapeDataString(fields)}";

        HttpResponseMessage response = await _httpClient.GetAsync(requestUrl);
        
        if (!response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
        {
            string errorContent = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
            throw new HttpRequestException($"Jira API call failed with status code {response.StatusCode}: {errorContent}");
        }

        string jsonResponse = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
        var result = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<JiraSearchResponseDto>(jsonResponse);
        
        return result?.Issues ?? new List<JiraIssueDto>();
    }
}

// --- Data Transfer Objects for Strict Deserialization ---

public class JiraSearchResponseDto
{
    [JsonPropertyName("issues")]
    public List<JiraIssueDto> Issues { get; set; } = new();
}

public class JiraIssueDto
{
    [JsonPropertyName("id")]
    public string Id { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [JsonPropertyName("key")]
    public string Key { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [JsonPropertyName("fields")]
    public IssueFields Fields { get; set; } = new();
}

public class IssueFields
{
    [JsonPropertyName("summary")]
    public string Summary { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [JsonPropertyName("description")]
    public object? Description { get; set; } // API v3 returns Atlassian Document Format (ADF) object

    [JsonPropertyName("issuetype")]
    public IssueType IssueType { get; set; } = new();
}

public class IssueType
{
    [JsonPropertyName("name")]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
4. Frontend Integration & Visual FX Pipeline
4.1 "The Portal Import" Animation Sequence
When the facilitator executes the Jira Import method, do not update the DOM instantly. Trigger this sequence:

The Portal: A CSS/Canvas rendering of a "Jira Blue Portal" vortex opens up in the center of the UI.

The Vacuum: Cards corresponding to the imported JiraIssueDto collection visually fly out of the vortex and cascade onto the poker table.

The Dust-Off: Because these items contain no points, apply a greyed-out, "dust-coated" CSS filter to the cards. The moment a player locks in their first vote on that card, shake the card asset via CSS and clear the filter to symbolize it "awakening."

4.2 SignalR Real-Time Wireframe Requirements
Implement a SignalR Hub (PokerHub.cs) to broadcast structural events globally:

SendVote(string userId, string cardValue) -> Syncs real-time visibility states.

TriggerDuel(string playerOneId, string playerTwoId) -> Directs the frontend to load the interactive split-screen.

CastArgumentSpell(string casterId, string spellType) -> Transmits localized particle effects across user views.

EnforceLavaTimer() -> Syncs the unified environmental countdown across client browsers.

5. Instructions for the Coding AI
When using this specification file to write code blocks:

Model Structuring: Ensure the data schemas handle properties for CurrentStreak, ScrumGoldBalance, and cosmetic item arrays mapped to individual player sessions.

Performance Isolation: Keep animation loops lightweight. Use CSS keyframes or requestAnimationFrame for the "Floor is Lava" or "Portal Import" logic so voting inputs remain responsive.

Robust Error Handling: Wrap the Jira API JSON deserialization to seamlessly catch instances where the user's Jira project utilizes specialized custom field IDs (customfield_XXXXX) instead of the literal string name "Story Points".
