<div class="group-card hard" id="group-k">
  <h3>Group K — 💾 Room Persistence <span class="plan-file">investigation-room-persistence.md</span></h3>
  <span class="tag">Hard</span><span class="tag">Program.cs · RoomService.cs · new IRoomRepository.cs · new SqliteRoomRepository.cs</span>

  <div class="callout info" style="margin-top:12px;">
    <strong>Investigation status</strong>
    Requires architectural decision on storage backend before implementation. See questions below.
  </div>

  <ul style="margin-top:10px;">
    <li><b>Problem:</b> <code>RoomService</code> is a singleton in-memory dictionary. A server restart (common on IONOS shared hosting) drops all rooms mid-sprint.</li>
    <li><b>Option 1 — SQLite:</b> Add <code>Microsoft.Data.Sqlite</code>. <code>IRoomRepository</code> with <code>SqliteRoomRepository</code>. Snapshot room state on every mutation (story add/complete, vote reveal). Simple schema: <code>rooms(name, json_blob, last_activity)</code>. On startup, hydrate from DB. TTL: auto-delete rooms inactive for 7 days.</li>
    <li><b>Option 2 — JSON file per room:</b> write <code>rooms/{name}.json</code> on mutation. Simpler, no dependency. Works on shared hosting without a DB engine. Less atomic, but room data is low-contention.</li>
    <li><b>Option 3 — Periodic snapshot only:</b> background timer every 60s writes dirty rooms to file. Lose up to 60s of state on crash. Easiest to implement.</li>
    <li><b>Recommendation:</b> Option 2 (JSON file per room, write-on-change) for IONOS compatibility. Wrap behind <code>IRoomRepository</code> so SQLite can be swapped in later. File path configurable via <code>appsettings.json</code>.</li>
    <li><b>In-scope:</b> room state (stories, participants, votes). Out-of-scope: vote history, analytics (those are client localStorage).</li>
    <li><b>Concurrency:</b> existing <code>lock(room)</code> pattern extends naturally — lock before write, <code>SemaphoreSlim</code> per room for async file I/O.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

